  "id": "Compound::DB09009 - Gene::6328",
  "verbalization": "Articaine, a local anesthetic, is linked to Scn3a through a series of mechanistic pathways that involve the binding of anesthetic agents to ion channels. The evidence suggests that Articaine, along with its analogs Ropivacaine and Mepivacaine, binds to Scn3a, a voltage-gated sodium channel critical for neuronal excitation. This binding is consistent across multiple paths, with the lowest common ancestor of Articaine and its analogs being the class of organooxygen compounds and anesthetic agents. The shared mechanism of action—binding to Scn3a—underpins the anesthetic effects observed in these compounds. The presence of a direct interaction between Articaine and Scn3a, as inferred from the evidence, supports a role in modulating neuronal activity, which is a key function of Scn3a. The evidence also highlights the importance of the anesthetic agent class in mediating these effects, reinforcing the mechanistic link between Articaine and Scn3a.",
